Biological Interpretation of Network Scores
EGFR as a Dominant Signaling Hub

EGFR exhibits the highest degree centrality in the network, confirming its role as the primary signaling hub. Its extensive connectivity indicates direct regulation of multiple downstream pathways controlling proliferation, survival, and resistance mechanisms.

This high centrality provides strong mechanistic justification for EGFR as a driver oncogene in LUAD.

Ligand Redundancy and Signal Robustness

Multiple ligands (EGF, TGFA, HBEGF, BTC) demonstrate moderate centrality, highlighting redundant receptor activation mechanisms. This redundancy explains persistent EGFR signaling even under partial pathway inhibition and contributes to therapeutic resistance.

Adaptor and Downstream Signaling Components

Adaptor proteins (GRB2, GAB1, SOS1) serve as signal transducers linking receptor activation to intracellular cascades. Downstream kinases (PIK3CA, PIK3R1) represent activation of the PI3K–AKT survival axis, a key oncogenic pathway in LUAD.

Mechanistic Insight

The network structure reveals a clear hierarchical organization:

Ligand-mediated EGFR activation

Adaptor-based signal amplification

Parallel activation of MAPK and PI3K pathways

EGFR occupies the highest regulatory tier, meaning that pharmacological inhibition at this level produces broad downstream disruption.

Safety and Risk Considerations

High network centrality is associated with both therapeutic benefit and potential risk:

Strong centrality explains clinical efficacy of EGFR inhibitors

Extensive pathway connectivity increases likelihood of on-target toxicity

Downstream redundancy supports emergence of resistance

These findings are consistent with known clinical observations, reinforcing the biological realism of the network model.

Validation Conclusion (GO / NO-GO)

Decision: GO — Mechanistically Supported Target

EGFR demonstrates:

dominant pathway integration

highest network centrality

direct control over oncogenic signaling modules

This systems-level evidence strongly supports EGFR as a mechanistically validated therapeutic target in LUAD.

Overall Conclusion

Pathway and network analysis confirms that EGFR functions as a central oncogenic regulator within LUAD signaling architecture. The high degree centrality, extensive ligand connectivity, and downstream pathway activation collectively explain both therapeutic efficacy and resistance behavior observed clinically. This mechanistic validation strengthens confidence in EGFR as a high-priority target for continued therapeutic development and combination strategy exploration.
